{
 "comment": "Fortran PARSEC (development version 1.4.x, gfortran 13.3) for small spin-polarized isolated systems (Spin_Polarization: .true.), read from parsec.out with Output_Level 2 in bohr, Ry and Ry/bohr with the digits Fortran prints. Every SCF loop is converged to 1e-8 Ry; 'iterations' is the number of SCF steps it took. 'eigenvalues', 'occupations', 'electrons' and 'energies' are those of the last step, per spin channel where there are two. 'initial_magnetic_moment' is what initchrg.f90 prints. 'forces' holds the tables of the structure: 'local' is \"local force components\" (ion-ion plus the local pseudopotential terms), 'ionic', 'sc' and 'core' its three pseudopotential terms. Pseudopotential files are named as tests/data/<file>: the spin_*_POTRE.DAT files are the potentials of the library under src/pp_generation as these runs read them, written by the converter while it resampled linearly (until October 2026). One case is a structural relaxation, with the steps of its atom.cor. A Python run needs Hartree_Boundary_Tolerance: off to use PARSEC's Hartree boundary values. With PBE the two programs differ beyond round-off: this package uses the potential of the discrete energy and Fortran the continuum expression (V_xc/pbe.py).",
